# `dataframe` — a table of data

`dataframe` makes a table of data from its column names and its rows, the way
a Stilo `data` cell writes it:

```
df: stilo.dataframe {
    columns: ["case", "Fx (kN)", "Fy (kN)"],
    rows: [
        ["ULS-1", 120, 35],
        ["ULS-2", 140, ""]
    ]
}
```

(between the tag delimiters, in a document that imported the library; see
`index.texl`). Labelled like this, the tag binds `df` and shows the table in
place.

- `columns` is a list of column names. They are texts, so any heading works,
  with spaces, units or symbols.
- `rows` is a list of rows, each a list of values, one per column. Any value
  can be computed: `["ULS-3", 1.35 * Fx_k, ""]`. An empty table cell is `""`
  (an empty value, `()`, would vanish from the list and shift the values after
  it). A row shorter than `columns` is padded with `""`; values beyond the
  last column are dropped.

A dataframe is a namespace:

- `df.columns` — the column names, a list.
- `df.rows` — the rows, a list of lists, each exactly as long as `columns`.
- `df.row i` — row `i`, counting from 0; nothing if there is no such row.
- `df.column name` — the values of the first column called `name`, a list;
  an undefined value if there is none.
- As text, it is a Markdown table: a header row with the column names, then
  one line per row. An empty value shows as an empty table cell, an undefined
  value as `[[Undefined]]` (it does not spoil the rest of the table), and
  other values as Texl writes them. A `|` in a value is escaped and a line
  break becomes a space, so the table stays whole. A column whose values are
  all numbers (empty and undefined ones aside) is aligned right.

Bad input (no namespace, `columns` or `rows` not a list, a row that is not a
list) gives an undefined value that says what is wrong, in a few words (Texl
shows a custom message of up to 24 characters in full).

The functions below are plain Swan. They use no recursion except `indices`,
whose depth is the number of columns.

<%
# A line break, for splitting and joining text.
NL = "
",

# The numbers 0 .. n - 1, as a list.
indices = n -> (n > 0) ? [list.enum (indices (n - 1)), n - 1] ; [],

# How many items of `values` (a list) satisfy `test`.
count = (test, values) -> list.size [(list.enum values) => (v -> test v ? 1)],

# A value as a tag shows it; never fails: an undefined value, or one that
# cannot be converted, is `[[Undefined]]`.
shown = text.template "{v}",

# A value as the text of a table cell: as shown, on one line, `|` escaped.
cell_text = v -> text.join "\|" (text.split "|" (text.join " " (text.split NL (shown {v: v})))),

# `row` cut or padded with "" to `n` values.
fit = (n, row) -> list.head n [list.enum row, (list.enum (indices n)) => (i -> "")],

# The values of column `i` of `rows` (rows already fitted), as a list.
column_at = (rows, i) -> [(list.enum rows) => (r -> r i)],

# Whether a column's values are all numbers, empty and undefined ones aside,
# with at least one.
numeric = values -> (
    numbers = count ((v -> type v == "Numb"), values),
    others = count ((v -> (type v != "Numb") & (type v != "Undefined") & (v != "")), values),
    (numbers > 0) & (others == 0)
),

# The two kinds of line of a Markdown table.
row_line = text.template "| {cells} |",
rule_line = text.template "|{marks}|",

# One row of the Markdown table: the header or a row of values.
table_line = values -> row_line {cells: text.join " | " ((list.enum values) => cell_text)},

# A dataframe as a Markdown table; the `__text__` hook of every dataframe.
table = df -> (list.size df.columns == 0) ? "" ; (
    marks = (list.enum (indices (list.size df.columns))) => (i -> numeric (column_at (df.rows, i)) ? "---:" ; "---"),
    text.join NL (table_line df.columns, rule_line {marks: text.join "|" marks}, (list.enum df.rows) => table_line)
),

# The dataframe of checked `columns` and `rows`.
make = (columns, rows) -> (
    n = list.size columns,
    fitted = [(list.enum rows) => (r -> fit (n, r))],
    {
        columns: columns,
        rows: fitted,
        row: i -> fitted i,
        column: name -> (
            i = list.find name columns,
            (i < 0) ? undef.from "no such column" ; column_at (fitted, i)
        ),
        __text__: table
    }
),

dataframe = spec ->
    (type spec != "Namespace") ? undef.from "not a namespace" ; (
    (type spec.columns != "List") ? undef.from "columns: not a list" ; (
    (type spec.rows != "List") ? undef.from "rows: not a list" ; (
    (count ((r -> type r != "List"), spec.rows) > 0) ? undef.from "a row is not a list" ;
    make (spec.columns, spec.rows)
)))
%>
